PUBLIC RESPONSE: {25 and 28}

The poem is a sestina whose six end-words rotate in a fixed order across its 12 stanzas (plus envoi). Labeling the end-words A=mountains, B=valleys, C=forests, D=music, E=morning, F=evening produces the canonical sequence for Strephon’s fifth stanza (lines 25-30): D-E-A-C-F-B.  

In the given text that stanza ends C-E-A-C-F-B instead: line 25 supplies the required D-word while line 28 supplies an extra C-word. The only minimal change that restores the prescribed pattern is to interchange those two lines; every other stanza already matches the sestina order exactly. Hence the swapped lines are 25 and 28.

12.text:
The poem is a double sestina whose strict form is defined by a fixed sequence of six end-words (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) that rotate according to the canonical rule 6-1-5-2-4-3 from stanza to stanza.  

Applying that rule stanza by stanza reproduces exactly the end-word order that appears in the given text for all twelve six-line stanzas. The only local textual rupture occurs inside the fifth Strephon stanza (lines 25-30). In that stanza the end-word sequence itself is still correct (D-E-A-C-F-B), yet the consecutive sentences  

“These forests eke, made wretched by our music;  
Hath made itself a crier of the morning”  

are grammatically discontinuous. The only pair of lines whose transposition both restores syntactic continuity and preserves the required end-word order is lines 26 and 28. After the swap the stanza reads  

… music;  
Long since my thoughts more desert be than forests;  
And hath with wailing strength climbed highest mountains;  
Hath made itself a crier of the morning;  
Long since I see my joys come to their evening;  
… valleys,  

which satisfies both the rotation rule and ordinary sentence logic. No other pair of lines produces this double restoration.

Thus the swapped lines are 26 and 28.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {26 and 28}

